WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.980
Applications do not only authenticate people.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:10.280
Services, jobs, containers, scripts, devices, and other workloads also need identity.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:20.300
Service accounts, API keys, client credentials, managed identities, certificates, and workload identity mechanisms allow machine-to-machine authentication.

4
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:29.760
The same IAM principles apply: each identity should be attributable, constrained, rotated, monitored, and limited to the access required for its job.

5
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:37.560
Shared long-lived credentials are risky because they are hard to rotate, hard to attribute, and often overprivileged.

6
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:43.580
If several services use the same key, logs may not show which workload actually performed an action.

7
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.720
If a key never expires, it can survive long after the original deployment changed.

8
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:55.540
If it is stored in source code, copied into tickets, or exposed through build logs, cleanup becomes painful.

9
00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:05.160
Managed identity and workload identity approaches can reduce secret handling by letting the platform issue short-lived credentials to approved workloads.

10
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.160
Developers should design service access around least privilege and rotation.

11
00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:20.580
API keys and client secrets should be stored in approved secret managers, scoped narrowly, rotated on a schedule and after suspected exposure, and removed when no longer needed.

12
00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.720
Avoid using human user credentials for automation.

13
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.940
Avoid one powerful service account for every integration.

14
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:33.920
Good workload identity design lets teams answer which service did what, when, from where, and with which permission set.
